oscience pervades our  society so in a 2014 study in the  scientific journal  one adults were shown before-and-after  photos of the same woman for a second  and a half sometimes they were the same  photo sometimes something was changed  about the woman maybe the color of her  hair whether she was wearing glasses  maybe her earrings changed the people  being tested were asked if the pictures  were the same or different and if they  noticed a difference they were asked  what the difference was mostly people  could correctly identify when there was  a change and when there wasn't but  what's interesting about the results was  that people were able to detect the  change even when they weren't able to  cognitively and consciously identify and  express what the change was and when the  participants were informed of the  results the fact that they were able to  detect changes even without being able  to explain what they were some of the  participants believed that they had some  kind of ESP because they intuitively  knew that something about the photo had  changed but they couldn't quite put  their finger on it and they said well  the reason is some kind of extrasensory  perception one of the study's co-authors  said quote people are correct in  believing that they have an ability to  sense changes in their environment even  when they cannot verbally identify what  those changes are it is just that this  ability is not a quasi magical Sixth  Sense it can be explained in terms of  known processes and this is a major  theme in pseudoscience humans want to  explain what they don't understand  often by any means necessary you might  not be conscious of what you're  perceiving so you might interpret your  correctness as something supernatural  there's a 2008 study at Harvard which  used brain scans to test whether people  claiming to have ESP were actually  acquiring knowledge that couldn't be  explained through some kind of normal  perception before the study scientists  already knew through neuroscience that  the brain responds a certain way when it  sees something familiar and a different  way when it sees something new before  the subjects were brain scanned they  were shown a bunch of pictures by way of  so called ESP what I mean by that is the  scientists would display the photos on a  computer screen in another another  for example or they would show the  photos to the subjects relative or  partner or friend in a different room  they displayed it in a number of other  ways that someone with ESP might claim  could get them to sense the image  without actually seeing it then they put  them in an fMRI brain scanner and showed  them the same pictures mixed with new  pictures that hadn't yet been used not  only could the subjects not identify  which pictures were new with any  statistical significance but their  brains responded the same way to every  photo responding the way a brain would  respond if it had never seen any of the  photos these studies aren't the full  extent of all research into ESP claims  but it's a sample of how we can actually  test some of these claims all the time  people report unexplained knowledge  having a dream where something happens  and the next day it happens in real life  or you were thinking about someone and  the very next moment you get an email  from them a dream or a feeling coming  true can seem or feel too amazing to be  a coincidence but not when you think  about it like a statistician you spend  your entire life constantly thinking  feeling and dreaming and every once in a  while some of those thoughts and  feelings line up with actual events it  would actually be more amazing if it  never happened much of the time it's  your brain putting things together  without you realizing it many  psychological processes go into what  makes up our intuitions and the  subconscious inferences we make every  day we aren't fully conscious of all the  perceptions we have and were rarely  conscious of what our 